If you have (IMO in Nethack quite well balanced) race/role combinations
and want to add some more then these should be different to the existing
races/roles and also have some characteristic properties, avoiding to be
just duplication and avoiding to be too easy or too hard to play. I think
this is a difficult task to do (cf. info section on the official Nethack
page: "We're still trying to get the existing roles differentiated.").

> (Fixed paths for the different combinations sounds as if there is much  
> variety in it, but there isn't really.)

I see two main design approaches; have a ([Nethack] comparable small(?))
number of races and roles, each race/role combination balanced as good
as possible to make every combination playable in a similar degree of
difficulty; or to have a large set which is hard to individually balance
but (maybe) coupled to certain properties (Nethack does this already to
a certain extent) without trying to balance them at any cost. The latter
may reflect more the Real Life based object models, where you just have
differences - some can play the Real Life game easily, while others are
just "Underdogs". Both design philosophies have their own interesting
properties for the gameplay.

I cannot speak for Crawl, whether they have succeeded to incorporate a
large set of well balanced races/roles; my impression is that there are
differences in difficulty to play, which may be intentional. (I don't
rate the two approaches.)

